
(Note that the below is a DRAFT version and is not finalized yet. It is
likely to be changed in future before it is implemented.)

See oid.ter for a list of OIDs and some other notes.


=== CATALOG.DER ===

The format of the CATALOG.DER lump is a ASN.1X DER sequence with fields:

* The game title, which is a PC string.

* Publication date (UTC-based timestamp).

* The set of authors. Each author is a set of the names of that author;
each name can be a PC String, TRON String, Visible String, or Object
Identifier. If it is a Visible String then it is interpreted as a URL
(any scheme is possible, e.g. "mailto:" for a email address). OIDs are
relative to base.1 (author identifiers).

* A long description, which is a PC string, or null. This text may
contain line breaks.

* Set of positive classifications (OIDs). (This means that the author
claims that these classifications do apply to this world.)

* Set of negative classifications (OIDs). (This means that the author
claims that these classifications do not apply to this world.)

* A OID to identify the hash algorithm. This may be relative to
2.16.840.1.101.3.4.2 (not to the Super ZZ Zero base OID).

* A set of Visible strings, which are URLs for downloading current or new
versions of the world file. This set may be empty, but if it is not empty
then all URLs must be direct download links. Any scheme may be used.

* A key/value list of extensions. The keys are OIDs and the values are
the values of those extensions.


=== Side file ===

The side file is also a DER file, and is a sequence with fields:

* Service identifier (a OID). This may be null if not applicable.

* A OID to identify the hash algorithm. This may be relative to
2.16.840.1.101.3.4.2 (not to the Super ZZ Zero base OID).

* The hash of the world file. The hash is stored as a octet string.

* A sequence, which is a copy of the sequence in the CATALOG.DER lump of
the world file.

* Set of positive classificaitions (OIDs). (This is like the set of
classifications in the CATALOG.DER lump, but what the service claims
instead of what the author claims.)

* Set of negative classifications (OIDs).

* A copy of the first field of GENERAL.DER (or an empty set if that
lump is not present in the world file).

* A copy of the second field of GENERAL.DER (or an empty set if that
lump is not present in the world file).

* A key/value list of extensions. The keys are OIDs and the values are
the values of those extensions. Some extensions are meaningful in both
the world file and side file, but some are only one or the other.

Object identifiers can be relative or absolute like CATALOG.DER; the
same rules are followed.

